# Autoconf site file for the aarch64-pc-cygwin cross build
# (loaded through CONFIG_SITE, set in .ci/makepkg-aarch64-cross.conf).
#
# The build machine is itself Windows on ARM64, so the programs configure
# compiles for the target can simply be run.  Without this, configure sees
# --build != --host, decides it is cross compiling and guesses every
# run-time check instead of testing it (279 guesses in coreutils alone).
# Some guesses swap in gnulib replacements that the native build never uses;
# one of them (fflush/fseeko on input streams) recursed on Cygwin, opening
# /dev/null until the descriptor table was full.
#
# With cross_compiling=maybe, configure's compiler check runs one test
# program; when it works, configure switches to native mode and runs every
# check on the ARM64 runtime itself, so the answers are the real ones for
# this target.  The ARM64 msys-2.0.dll and the target libraries are found
# through a directory of ARM64 DLLs at the end of PATH (see
# crossarm64/20-build-userland.sh): the Windows loader skips a DLL built for
# another machine and keeps searching, so the x86-64 build tools still load
# their own DLLs from /usr/bin.
if test "$cross_compiling" = yes; then
  cross_compiling=maybe
fi

# gettext's and libiconv's search macros (AM_ICONV, AM_GNU_GETTEXT) look in
# $prefix, i.e. the build machine's /usr, find the x86-64 libiconv/libintl
# there, fail to link them and drop iconv and NLS support (issue #52).  The
# target's libraries are in the sysroot.  Set unconditionally: a native
# recipe's --without-libiconv-prefix means "use the default prefix", which
# for this build is the sysroot.
with_libiconv_prefix=/usr/aarch64-pc-cygwin/usr
with_libintl_prefix=/usr/aarch64-pc-cygwin/usr
